<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rashid al-Din Hamadani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">executed <a href="/years/1318">1318</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Rashid al-Din</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rashid al-Din Hamadani was a Persian physician, statesman and historian who served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>. He carried out the extensive reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazan-khan">Ghazan Khan</a> and wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jami">Jami</a> al-Tawarikh, a universal history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a>. He was executed in 1318, depriving the Ilkhanate of its most capable administrator.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani.jpg" alt="Rashid al-Din Hamadani" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jami al-Tawarikh artist, 1420-1430 artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Presentation_of_the_book,_folio_6_(Biblioth%C3%A8que_nationale_de_France,_Suppl%C3%A9ment_Persan_1113).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Rashid al-Din</b>'s Jami al-Tawarikh, written at the Ilkhanid court with Mongol, Chinese, Persian and European informants, is often called the first universal history of Eurasia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mongols influenced successor states by combining steppe traditions with sedentary administration, above all in Yuan China and the Ilkhanate of Persia, where <b>Rashid al-Din</b> compiled a pioneering world history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Persian prose includes histories, such as the world history of <b>Rashid al-Din</b> in the 14th century, travel accounts, mirrors for princes, and the memoirs of rulers; Middle Persian had already transmitted to the Islamic world the Indian fables of the Panchatantra, as Kalila and Dimna.</blockquote><a href="/language/persian">Persian · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>In Iran the Ilkhan Ghazan became a Muslim in 1295, and his minister <b>Rashid al-Din</b> compiled the Compendium of Chronicles, an attempt at a history of the whole known world that drew on Chinese, Persian, Arabic, Turkic, Indian, and European sources and informants.</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.8,48.52&amp;year=1318" aria-label="Hamadan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5781 904 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rashid al-Din Hamadani"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6231 1174h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6231 1174h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.8,48.52&amp;year=1318">What was here in 1318? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
